    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green (34 tests, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the string membership tier with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133), computed and stored on owner creation.\n\n**Level formula** (in `OwnerRestControllerV1.membershipLevelFor`): start at 1, +1 when an email is present, +1 when `namesakeCount` is 0, capped at 3 (level 4 reserved for tenure).\n\nChanges:\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 added `membershipLevelFor(...)` helper, set the level during `addOwner` (after email/namesakeCount are set), and extended the create audit line to record `membershipLevel={}` alongside the owner id (replacing the tier).\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the persisted `membershipLevel` field (`membership_level` column) with getter/setter.\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 removed the read-time `membershipTier` GOLD/SILVER/BRONZE computed mapping; `membershipLevel` now maps automatically by name from the stored value.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 replaced the `membershipTier` string schema with a `membershipLevel` integer (min 1, max 3), which regenerates the DTO so the response exposes `membershipLevel` and no longer `membershipTier`.\n- **DB schemas** (hsqldb, h2, postgres, mysql) \u2014 added the `membership_level` column to the `owners` table.\n\nThe prior `GOLD` tier that depended on a 3-member household is gone; household size no longer affects the level (a third household member with a unique name and no email is now level 2, matching the spec and tests).",
